 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "**Mechanism:** werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects reading one `ContextVar`. Each proxy looks up the current `AppContext` in the context variable at access time and returns an attribute of it.\n\n**In `src/tessel/globals.py`:**\n- `_app_var: ContextVar[AppContext]` (`\"tessel.app_ctx\"`) holds the current context for the running thread or async task.\n- `active_app = LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")` resolves to `AppContext.app`, the `Tessel` instance.\n- `g = LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")` resolves to `AppContext.g`, an `_AppCtxGlobals`.\n- `request = LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")` resolves to `AppContext.request`, a `Request`.\n- `app_ctx` (the context itself) and `session` are proxied the same way.\n- If no context is set, the proxy raises its `unbound_message` (\"Working outside of application context\" or \"…request context\").\n\n**In `src/tessel/ctx.py`:**\n- `AppContext.push()` (line ~416) calls `_app_var.set(self)` and stores the token in `_cv_token`.\n- `AppContext.pop()` (line ~446) calls `_app_var.reset(self._cv_token)`, restoring the previous context.\n- Helpers like `has_request_context` / `has_app_context` read `_app_var.get(None)`.\n\n`request` and `session` only work when the pushed `AppContext` was built with a request (`ctx.has_request`). Otherwise they raise the request-context error.",
